<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goa Gajah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Elephant Cave</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> Gajah ('Elephant Cave') is a sanctuary near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedulu">Bedulu</a> in Gianyar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, dating from around the 11th century. Its rock-cut cave is entered through a carved mouth of a demonic face, and it contains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> images, while Buddhist remains lie nearby. Bathing pools fed by springs were excavated in front of the cave in the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goa-gajah.jpg" alt="Goa Gajah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chainwit. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bali_-_Goa_Gajah_(2022)_-_img_20.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
